Title: Load an .ico whose 256px image Windows XP cannot decode
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

On Windows XP, creating a Tray (or anything else) from an ordinary .ico path
failed:

  Could not create the tray icon: Error: Failed to load image from path
  'Z:\dlls\sample_app\src\imgs\icon.ico'

The file is fine. It holds four images -- 16, 32 and 48 pixels as bitmaps, and
256 pixels PNG-compressed, which is how every icon editor has written that
size since Vista introduced the format. XP's icon loader predates it and
cannot decode the entry.

NativeImage's constructor for an .ico path builds its fallback image from
GetHICON(256) and nothing else. On XP that LoadImage call fails, the image is
left empty, and TryConvertNativeImage reports the path as unloadable -- though
the sizes XP would actually have used, 16 for the tray and 32 for a caption,
are sitting in the same file undamaged.

So the constructor now tries 256, then 48, 32 and 16, and takes the first that
loads. Nothing changes where 256 works: the loop stops at its first iteration.
GetHICON already loads each requested size from the file separately, so the
tray still gets its real 16-pixel image and not a scaled-down fallback.

diff --git a/electron/shell/common/api/electron_api_native_image.cc b/electron/shell/common/api/electron_api_native_image.cc
--- a/electron/shell/common/api/electron_api_native_image.cc
+++ b/electron/shell/common/api/electron_api_native_image.cc
@@ -167,9 +167,22 @@
     : hicon_path_(hicon_path), isolate_(isolate) {
   EnsureSupportedScaleFactors();
 
-  // Use the 256x256 icon as fallback icon.
+  // Use the 256x256 icon as fallback icon -- or, where that one cannot be
+  // had, the largest that can.
+  //
+  // The 256x256 image in a modern .ico is stored PNG-compressed, a form
+  // Windows Vista introduced. Windows XP cannot decode it, so LoadImage fails
+  // for that size there, and with an empty fallback image the whole path is
+  // reported as "Failed to load image from path" -- for an icon whose
+  // 16, 32 and 48 pixel images are perfectly loadable and are the ones a tray
+  // icon or a window caption would have used anyway. So try those before
+  // giving up. On a Windows that reads the PNG form the first attempt
+  // succeeds and the rest never run.
   gfx::ImageSkia image_skia;
-  electron::util::ReadImageSkiaFromICO(&image_skia, GetHICON(256));
+  for (const int size : {256, 48, 32, 16}) {
+    if (electron::util::ReadImageSkiaFromICO(&image_skia, GetHICON(size)))
+      break;
+  }
   image_ = gfx::Image(image_skia);
 
   UpdateExternalAllocatedMemoryUsage();
